# Three.js InstancedMesh Baking

## Purpose
Convert `THREE.InstancedMesh` into regular meshes so exporters that ignore instances can preserve per-instance transforms.

## Setup
- Requires `three` (e.g., `npm install three`).
- Works in Node.js or browser environments.

## Usage Pattern
```js
import * as THREE from 'three';

function expandInstancedMesh(instanced) {
  const meshes = [];
  const tempMatrix = new THREE.Matrix4();

  for (let i = 0; i < instanced.count; i++) {
    instanced.getMatrixAt(i, tempMatrix);
    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(instanced.geometry, instanced.material);
    mesh.name = `${instanced.name || 'instance'}_${i}`;
    mesh.applyMatrix4(tempMatrix);
    meshes.push(mesh);
  }

  return meshes;
}
```

## Notes
- Apply the instance matrix to the mesh, not the geometry, if you want to preserve normals as the exporter handles `matrixWorld`.
- If you need merged geometry for performance, clone and apply matrix to geometry, then merge.
- Call `instanced.updateMatrixWorld(true)` before extracting matrices when parent transforms matter.
